  @.2304 text "Two HTTP URIs that are equivalent after normalization (using any method) can be assumed to identify the same resource, and any HTTP component MAY perform normalization. As a result, distinct resources SHOULD NOT be identified by HTTP URIs that are equivalent after normalization (using any method defined in "
  @.2309 link "Section 6.2" url="/rfc/rfc3986#section-6.2"
  @.2311 text " of ["
  @.2312 link "URI" url=$u1
  @.2314 text "])."
  @.2316 link "4.2.4. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-4.2.4"
  @.2318 link "Deprecation of userinfo in http(s) URIs" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-deprecation-of-userinfo-in-"
  @.2320 text "The URI generic syntax for authority also includes a userinfo subcomponent (["
  @.2322 link "URI" url=$u1
  @.2324 text "], "
  @.2325 link "Section 3.2.1" url="/rfc/rfc3986#section-3.2.1"
  @.2327 text ") for including user authentication information in the URI. In that subcomponent, the use of the format "user:password" is deprecated."
  @.2328 text "Some implementations make use of the userinfo component for internal configuration of authentication information, such as within command invocation options, configuration files, or bookmark lists, even though such usage might expose a user identifier or password.A sender MUST NOT"
  @.2331 text " generate the userinfo subcomponent (and its "@" delimiter) when an "http" or "https" URI reference is generated within a message as a target URI or field value."
  @.2332 text "Before making use of an "http" or "https" URI reference received from an untrusted source, a recipient "
  @.2333 text "SHOULD parse for userinfo and treat its presence as an error; it is likely being used to obscure the authority for the sake of phishing attacks."
  @.2335 link "4.2.5. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-4.2.5"
  @.2337 link "http(s) References with Fragment Identifiers" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-https-references-with-fragm"
  @.2339 text "Fragment identifiers allow for indirect identification of a secondary resource, independent of the URI scheme, as defined in "
  @.2340 link "Section 3.5" url="/rfc/rfc3986#section-3.5"
  @.2342 text " of ["
  @.2343 link "URI" url=$u1
  @.2345 text "]. Some protocol elements that refer to a URI allow inclusion of a fragment, while others do not. They are distinguished by use of the ABNF rule for elements where fragment is allowed; otherwise, a specific rule that excludes fragments is used.Note: The fragment identifier component is not part of the scheme definition for a URI scheme (see "
  @.2349 link "Section 4.3" url="/rfc/rfc3986#section-4.3"
  @.2351 text " of ["
  @.2352 link "URI" url=$u1
  @.2354 text "]"
  @.2355 text "), thus does not appear in the ABNF definitions for the "http" and "https" URI schemes above."
  @.2356 link "4.3. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-4.3"
  @.2358 link "Authoritative Access" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-authoritative-access"
  @.2360 text "Authoritative access refers to dereferencing a given identifier, for the sake of access to the identified resource, in a way that the client believes is authoritative (controlled by the resource owner). The process for determining whether access is granted is defined by the URI scheme and often uses data within the URI components, such as the authority component when the generic syntax is used. However, authoritative access is not limited to the identified mechanism."
  @.2361 link "Section 4.3.1" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#origin"
  @.2363 text " defines the concept of an origin as an aid to such uses, and the subsequent subsections explain how to establish that a peer has the authority to represent an origin.See "
  @.2365 link "Section 17.1" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#establishing.authority"
  @.2367 text " for security considerations related to establishing authority."
  @.2368 link "4.3.1. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-4.3.1"
  @.2370 link "URI Origin" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-uri-origin"
  @.2372 text "The "origin" for a given URI is the triple of scheme, host, and port after normalizing the scheme and host to 
