  @.2655 text "]).A client MAY discard or truncate received field lines that are larger than the client wishes to process if the field semantics are such that the dropped value(s) can be safely ignored without changing the message framing or response semantics."
  @.2660 link "5.5. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-5.5"
  @.2662 link "Field Values" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-field-values"
  @.2664 text "HTTP field values consist of a sequence of characters in a format defined by the field's grammar. Each field's grammar is usually defined using ABNF (["
  @.2666 link "RFC5234" url=$u1
  @.2668 text "])."
  @.2670 text "  field-value    = *field-content
  field-content  = field-vchar
                   [ 1*( SP / HTAB / field-vchar ) field-vchar ]
  field-vchar    = VCHAR / obs-text
  obs-text       = %x80-FF
"
  @.2671 text "A field value does not include leading or trailing whitespace. When a specific version of HTTP allows such whitespace to appear in a message, a field parsing implementation MUST exclude such whitespace prior to evaluating the field value.Field values are usually constrained to the range of US-ASCII characters ["
  @.2676 link "USASCII" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#USASCII"
  @.2678 text "]. Fields needing a greater range of characters can use an encoding, such as the one defined in ["
  @.2681 link "RFC8187" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#RFC8187"
  @.2683 text "]. Historically, HTTP allowed field content with text in the ISO-8859-1 charset ["
  @.2686 link "ISO-8859-1" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#ISO-8859-1"
  @.2688 text "], supporting other charsets only through use of ["
  @.2691 link "RFC2047" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#RFC2047"
  @.2693 text "] encoding. Specifications for newly defined fields SHOULD limit their values to visible US-ASCII octets (VCHAR), SP, and HTAB. A recipient SHOULD treat other allowed octets in field content (i.e., "
  @.2699 link "obs-text" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#fields.values"
  @.2701 text ") as opaque data.Field values containing CR, LF, or NUL characters are invalid and dangerous, due to the varying ways that implementations might parse and interpret those characters; a recipient of CR, LF, or NUL within a field value MUST either reject the message or replace each of those characters with SP before further processing or forwarding of that message. Field values containing other CTL characters are also invalid; however, recipients MAY retain such characters for the sake of robustness when they appear within a safe context (e.g., an application-specific quoted string that will not be processed by any downstream HTTP parser)."
  @.2707 text "Fields that only anticipate a single member as the field value are referred to as "singleton fields"."
  @.2708 text "Fields that allow multiple members as the field value are referred to as "list-based fields". The list operator extension of "
  @.2709 link "Section 5.6.1" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#abnf.extension"
  @.2711 text " is used as a common notation for defining field values that can contain multiple members."
  @.2712 text "Because commas (",") are used as the delimiter between members, they need to be treated with care if they are allowed as data within a member. This is true for both list-based and singleton fields, since a singleton field might be erroneously sent with multiple members and detecting such errors improves interoperability. Fields that expect to contain a comma within a member, such as within an "
  @.2713 link "HTTP-date" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#http.date"
  @.2715 text " or "
  @.2716 link "URI-reference" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#uri.references"
  @.2718 text " element, ought to be defined with delimiters around that element to distinguish any comma within that data from potential list separators.For example, a textual date and a URI (either of which might contain a comma) could be safely carried in list-based field values like these:"
  @.2720 text "Example-URIs: "http://example.com/a.html,foo",
              "http://without-a-comma.example.com/"
Example-Dates: "Sat, 04 May 1996", "Wed, 14 Sep 2005
... [tee capped at 4096 of 12005 bytes]

