  @.3941 text "["
  @.3942 link "Err5433" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#Err5433"
  @.3944 text "])."
  @.3946 link "8.3.3. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-8.3.3"
  @.3948 link "Multipart Types" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-multipart-types"
  @.3950 text "MIME provides for a number of "multipart" types -- encapsulations of one or more representations within a single message body. All multipart types share a common syntax, as defined in "
  @.3951 link "Section 5.1.1" url="/rfc/rfc2046#section-5.1.1"
  @.3953 text " of ["
  @.3954 link "RFC2046" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#RFC2046"
  @.3956 text "], and include a boundary parameter as part of the media type value. The message body is itself a protocol element; a sender MUST generate only CRLF to represent line breaks between body parts."
  @.3960 text "HTTP message framing does not use the multipart boundary as an indicator of message body length, though it might be used by implementations that generate or process the content. For example, the "multipart/form-data" type is often used for carrying form data in a request, as described in "
  @.3961 text "["
  @.3962 link "RFC7578" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#RFC7578"
  @.3964 text "]"
  @.3965 text ", and the "multipart/byteranges" type is defined by this specification for use in some "
