  @.5504 text ") differs from Location in that the Content-Location refers to the most specific resource corresponding to the enclosed representation. It is therefore possible for a response to contain both the Location and Content-Location header fields."
  @.5505 link "10.2.3. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-10.2.3"
  @.5507 link "Retry-After" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-retry-after"
  @.5509 text "Servers send the "Retry-After" header field to indicate how long the user agent ought to wait before making a follow-up request. When sent with a "
  @.5510 link "503 (Service Unavailable)" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#status.503"
  @.5512 text " response, Retry-After indicates how long the service is expected to be unavailable to the client. When sent with any "
  @.5513 link "3xx (Redirection)" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#status.3xx"
  @.5515 text " response, Retry-After indicates the minimum time that the user agent is asked to wait before issuing the redirected request.The Retry-After field value can be either an HTTP-date or a number of seconds to delay after receiving the response."
  @.5517 text "  Retry-After = HTTP-date / delay-seconds
"
  @.5518 text "A delay-seconds value is a non-negative decimal integer, representing time in seconds."
  @.5519 text "  delay-seconds  = 1*DIGIT
"
  @.5520 text "Two examples of its use are"
  @.5521 text "Retry-After: Fri, 31 Dec 1999 23:59:59 GMT
Retry-After: 120
"
  @.5522 text "In the latter example, the delay is 2 minutes."
  @.5523 link "10.2.4. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-10.2.4"
  @.5525 link "Server" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-server"
  @.5527 text "The "Server" header field contains information about the software used by the origin server to handle the request, which is often used by clients to help identify the scope of reported interoperability problems, to work around or tailor requests to avoid particular server limitations, and for analytics regarding server or operating system use. An origin server "
  @.5528 text "MAY generate a Server header field in its responses."
  @.5530 text "  Server = product *( RWS ( product / comment ) )
"
  @.5531 text "The Server header field value consists of one or more product identifiers, each followed by zero or more comments ("
  @.5532 link "Section 5.6.5" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#comments"
  @.5534 text "), which together identify the origin server software and its significant subproducts. By convention, the product identifiers are listed in decreasing order of their significance for identifying the origin server software. Each product identifier consists of a name and optional version, as defined in "
  @.5535 link "Section 10.1.5" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#field.user-agent"
  @.5537 text ".Example:"
  @.5539 text "Server: CERN/3.0 libwww/2.17
"
  @.5540 text "An origin server SHOULD NOT generate a Server header field containing needlessly fine-grained detail and SHOULD limit the addition of subproducts by third parties. Overly long and detailed Server field values increase response latency and potentially reveal internal implementation details that might make it (slightly) easier for attackers to find and exploit known security holes."
  @.5545 link "11. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-11"
  @.5547 link "HTTP Authentication" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-http-authentication"
  @.5549 link "11.1. " url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-11.1"
  @.5551 link "Authentication Scheme" url="/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-authentication-scheme"
  @.5553 text "HTTP provides a general framework for access control and authentication, via an extensible set of challenge-response authentication schemes, which can be used by a server to challenge a client request and by a client to provide authentication information. It uses a case-insensitive token to identify the authentication scheme:"
  @.5554 text "  auth-scheme    = token
"
  @.5555 text "Aside from the general framework, this document does not specify any authentication schemes. New and existing authentication schemes are specified independently and ought to be registered with
... [tee capped at 4096 of 11562 bytes]

